{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/284289-how-to-separate-yourself-from-your-character","id":284289,"title":"How to separate yourself from your character","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-10-16T15:32:07-07:00","id":284289,"title":"How to separate yourself from your character","content":"Hi there.<br /> <br /> First of all, I'd like to say two things:<br /> <br /> 1] English isn't my native language so at times I may sound strange or my message may not be very clear. I apologize in advance for that.<br /> <br /> 2] Do not write anything if you intend on mocking me. I am mocking myself enough, I really don't need your &quot;support&quot; in that case. Unless mocking someone who you've never met over the Internet makes you feel very good about yourself, then who am I to deny you that.<br /> <br /> <br /> My question is... How do you separate yourself from your character when playing WoD? And is it really an issue for you at all, or am I just... weird?<br /> <br /> The thing is... some of my best friends play WoD regularly, and they are really nice, mature people who are great to play with. They don't seem to mind me, and I respect and like them a lot, so it would be nice to spend some time together when playing.<br /> <br /> I also like the setting very much - the closeness between the world of the game and the real world gives it this... sort of a &quot;real-ness&quot; feeling, makes you very engaged in the play, makes you feel very... strong emotions, as it is much easier to relate to the events that take place in the world that is mostly like ours, only with some &quot;additions&quot;. And that's great, I think. Especially for people who, like me, play mainly for the narrative.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> But that's... kind of a problem. For me at least. Whenever I try to play WoD, I just end up creating a supernatural version of myself. Or - in the least - the idealized, supernatural version of who I'd like to be but am not. And that... well, to me, that sucks. 'Cause when playing, I am... sort of constantly looking at myself from the side. And I don't really... like the things I see. Suffice to say, at the moment I don't... have a high opinion of myself. Worse still, I feel... exposed. Like... I am worried that the other people see what I see what I look at my character - which really, more often than not is just me - and see what I see. And then think less of me.<br /> <br /> <br /> I said that I tend to create either clones of myself, or idealized versions of myself. The latter is only partially true. The thing is... I can't seem to be able to roleplay a character I'd REALLY want to be like in real life, just because... well... I am not like this. Example: I, in real life, am a very quiet, shy person. And I find it impossible to roleplay as an outgoing, boisterous guy 'cause even if I wanted to, I don't really know how those people act. I think it would seem... artificial. And pathetic. But then, so is my own image - at least in my own eyes - and that's what I'm stuck with.<br /> <br /> <br /> Does anyone have this kind of problem as well? What did you do with it? (Also, as a half-serious note: do you feel stupid as well when your GM sets the base of the campaign on some historical events/seemingly common knowledge about the world in general, and you find out that you don't really know much about any of these things?)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4107-littledwarf","name":"LittleDwarf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-16T15:50:50-07:00","id":284299,"title":"","content":"Hi there. First of all, I can reliably say this: No, you're not the only one who has this &quot;issue&quot; (if you want to call it that).<br /> <br /> In fact, in my experience, most people, when they start getting into roleplaying, do one of two things:<br /> <br /> 1. Create characters very like themself, most often idealized versions<br /> <br /> 2. Create characters completely different from themselves, often with characteristics they wished they had<br /> <br /> Neither of those is wrong in any way, of course, roleplaying is an art, and not everyone takes to it equally fast, starting out from familiar ground, so to speak is actually a pretty good idea. However I must admit I do grow a bit tired when people refuse to go outside those initial boundaries, and essentially just create the same character over and over.<br /> <br /> Personally, I fit into category #1 as well when I started out, the strong, silent type was my go-to character (not that I was particularly strong, that would be the &quot;idealized&quot; part of it).<br /> <br /> So, what did I do with it ? Well, to be frank, I forced myself, with the aid of some very good STs, to play outside my &quot;safe zone&quot; of the strong, silent &quot;warrior&quot; type. I still remember my first ever W:tA chronicle, where the ST decided I was to play a Ragabash (the players were all complete noobs to the game, so the ST simply decided for us). Of course, this necessitates a complete level of trust within the group, and a high tolerance for people &quot;not playing out their role&quot; (I do so hate that expression).<br /> <br /> Finally, I have just one piece of advice: Roleplaying is about having fun, together with friends. So what if you play a charismatic character as a bit of a stereotype ? The only way it would be &quot;pathetic&quot; is if someone in your group made it out to be that way, and if they are indeed your friends, they wouldn't do that. I'm sure my first charismatic characters were total cartoons or b-movie heroes, but who cares? I got to play out a side of myself I pretty much never showed any other time, and in the long run, I do believe it helped me become a more charismatic person as a whole. After all, practice makes perfect, in any field of life. If you want to be strong, you have to hit those weights, if you want to be more charismatic, you have to try that, too. And what better arena than with people you (hopefully) trust not to take a piss on your expense? That is the beauty of roleplaying, and one of the main reasons I love this hobby.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/300-hod","name":"HOD","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar300_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"466"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-16T16:04:23-07:00","id":284309,"title":"","content":"I have penchant for creating PCs that are modeled partly on a fictional character or character hook from other RP settings, but given a proper twist and tweaking to make it a stand-alone character.<br /> <br /> That's my approach to my work in progress Changeling: Lost PC.<br /> <br /> Also given I use either Roll 20 or post by post, I have no issue with playing different genders, I can detach myself. Thankfully, sexual orientation is not a problem given my versatile... inclinations.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u201cI am absolute, I am perfect, I am supreme. I shall be eternal. My tragedy, is that there is no other fate for me. My powerlessness was that I couldn\u2019t subjugate my journey to the gods, while dreaming of rebirth at the end of distant time, like other pharaohs.\u201d Ramesses II, Fate/Prototype: Argent Fragments.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2918-kat","name":"Kat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2918_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1762"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-16T17:07:01-07:00","id":284354,"title":"","content":"I think that the best way to get out of the mold you seem to be in (and forgive me if I misinterpreted your post, since it seems like you do) is to pick a fictional character. Preferably one that you have seen on a long running show or anime or what have you or else someone who has been in a series of movies. Pick them and try to play them. Even if all you did was to copy them and change a name. For example, if you wanted to play a cocky arrogant genius billionaire who has black hair and a goatee but named him Loni Dark, then go for it! It helps you to ease your way out of the rut and to try different things. If your ST and your group are your friends (and they should be since you spend a few nights with them for hours on end), then they may give you some friendly joking but no one will (or should) begrudge you trying it out. <br /> When you do this a few times, then you will be able to make new characters who are not copies of others and not the same that you have been playing. One day, you will be able to make characters wholly from your mind and be able to slip into them easily. I tend to make new characters be nothing like the previous characters I have played because I enjoy the mental exercise, however my first character ever was a human fighter in AD&amp;D. When he died, I made up a series of characters just like him until I broke the mold with a Paladin who had a different personality.<br /> Ease yourself out of the rut by borrowing or even stealing other personalities or characters from other media and then use those training wheels to help you with making up brand new people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-17T05:17:54-07:00","id":284857,"title":"","content":"I realize not everyone can be satisfied with this, but I create each character to be someone I would be able to see physiologically destroyed. For me, World of Darkness is about losing. This usually helps me get into the character so I can try to play out their twisted/selfish motivations, without being &quot;angry&quot; when they die.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3920-sarah-rps","name":"Sarah RPs","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3920_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"38"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-17T07:23:15-07:00","id":284936,"title":"","content":"I love making characters, I do it a lot. <br /> <br /> What you describe isn't a &quot;problem&quot; so much as it is the beginning of a wonderful boon. What follows is my personal process, so please take it with a grain of salt.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Instead of doing an idealized you (or a diametric opposite), try to instead find two personality traits (preferably not ones that work well together) from your own psyche, and then combine them into the mind of a new psyche. Meaning... make these two ideas the character's foundation.<br /> <br /> So for example of a recent character, I wanted to combine the concept of an outsider (loner type) with the concept of someone who was driven to help people (at great risk to himself).<br /> <br /> What I like to do is take these two things (I choose two because its an easy foundation, 1 or 3 or 15 could work depending on complexity), which are obviously some part of my own mind (probably not the most dominate parts) and just sit on them for a day. <br /> <br /> It's also sometimes VERY helpful to consider a possible flawed concept... like in the previous example, I chose to make this character a racist (supernatural wise).<br /> <br /> Go about your normal daily business, and try to imagine having MET a person who was fundamentally driven by those concepts. Throw out ideas that dont seem right and let your mind wander over all the people you see. Their clothing, their mannerisms, their little foibles that most people dont see - all with an eye not of ridicule or judgement, but personality theft! <br /> <br /> Usually by the end of the day I have a character who has some of it's own personality beginning to sort of form. Through the &quot;character creation&quot; process of slapping down dots or stats or LARP notes or whatever it is you use as a record, you will flesh them out and get some more details.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The reason I like using this method is because I find it leads to characters (NPCs and PCs) that grow, learn, change, and are not me. They all have their true roots in my psyche, enough that I can explore the aspects without losing myself in the process.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/PbrO9Kq.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2211-sixfootchicken","name":"SixFootChicken","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2211_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"559"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-17T08:52:00-07:00","id":284984,"title":"","content":"I make my character's hilariously flawed to a degree that they're nothing like me. Makes it really easy to separate myself from them. I'd recommend practicing your character before you make it. Make a concept and build the sheet around it and all","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1VN49Y7tH2ZV0l7Hx65RWX1ez-Q9Lst-8ZTyTJeCTpxM/\" target=\"_blank\">Sorcerer 20th</a> homebrew and my update to <a href=\"http://immortalthegathering.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Highlander: the Gathering</a> for 20th Anniversary edition.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2414-theoutlander523","name":"theoutlander523","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"238"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-17T09:01:38-07:00","id":285002,"title":"","content":"&quot;Me, but with supernatural powers&quot; is how a lot of people start RPing. It's easy, it's fun, and it doesn't require a lot of thought. Nothing wrong with it.<br /> <br /> If you want to do something else, you'll just need practice. Essentially, you need to make up a character with passions, motivations, and a history you don't share, and while you're playing the game, you ask yourself &quot;what would make sense for a person with these traits to do?&quot;<br /> <br /> Here's an example. Suppose my character is in a bar with some guy who was harassed my character's friends.<br /> <br /> If my character were based on me, they would try to talk it out. They'd try to figure out the source of the guy's beef, and resolve it. Failing that, they might try using stern language or veiled threats to convey that a fight might be coming if the guy didn't lay off. They would probably call for support if the guy actually looked like he'd be willing to fight. They would mostly be concerned about the long-term consequences of any conflict.<br /> <br /> If my character were a hot-blooded warrior from a culture with a strong honor code and a fierce devotion to protecting his friends, they would be a lot more hostile from the word go. They might punch the guy in the face and demand a duel without even asking for the guy's name. They would mostly be concerned about immediate threats to their friends and upholding their honor.<br /> <br /> It could be some time before you're ready to do this automatically, but improvised roleplaying is almost entirely a matter of asking &quot;What would (Character) do?&quot; and then doing that thing.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-17T11:30:07-07:00","id":285168,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sarah RPs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/284289-how-to-separate-yourself-from-your-character?p=284857#post284857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I realize not everyone can be satisfied with this, but I create each character to be someone I would be able to see physiologically destroyed. For me, World of Darkness is about losing. This usually helps me get into the character so I can try to play out their twisted/selfish motivations, without being &quot;angry&quot; when they die.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This is good advice. Don't necessarily make characters you hate, but build your character with story hooks based on failures. If you build a character with the assumption that they will succeed, then it's harder to deal with it when they don't. If you build a character with the assumption that they will fail in all the worst ways, then you can really get into trying to save them from it--and still enjoy the story of them failing anyway.<br /> <br /> Most WoD books (or any rpgs lately, really) have a bunch of questions in character generation that you can ask yourself to flesh out your character. Add to these questions: How could this character die heroically, how could this character be corrupted and turned into someone they hate, how could you see this character dying like a coward.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2214-traveller","name":"Traveller","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2214_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-18T11:07:16-07:00","id":286040,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Sarah RPs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/284289-how-to-separate-yourself-from-your-character?p=284857#post284857\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I realize not everyone can be satisfied with this, but I create each character to be someone I would be able to see physiologically destroyed. For me, World of Darkness is about losing. This usually helps me get into the character so I can try to play out their twisted/selfish motivations, without being &quot;angry&quot; when they die.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well... That may be a good advice from a technical point of view, but... Well. The list of people I'd actually be able to see &quot;physiologicaly&quot; (or psychologicaly for that matter, as there is a major difference and I don't know which of these you meant) is rather short. And I would certainly not enjoy roleplaying such character.<br /> <br /> There are some things that nearly always come up as a part of my character's identity - and many people, including my friends with whom I play - have tried (with mixed success) to hammer it into my head that that's not inherently bad thing. And maybe I agree with them.<br /> <br /> What I want to achieve is not to play a character that is my exact oposite or that has the moral outlook on the world that is critically incompatibile with my own. That would simply not be fun for me, not to say that it would be very difficult to pull of, as I would need to constantly remind myself about things such as for example &quot;I regard honesty and honor a naive lies&quot;, while in reality it's the complete opposite.<br /> <br /> I'd like to create a character that has mostly the same moral views as I do, but a distinctly different personality, so that it wouldn't instantly be reckognized as &quot;LittleDwarf with superpowers!&quot; A confident, out-spoken guy, for example. But I don't really... Well... I don't know if any of you actually understand that, but when I tried, I found out that I - in all seriousnes, out-of-character, in real life - actually DON'T KNOW what should be said or done in some situations (I am frequently left speechless when the conversations hit certain topics I am not used to speak about, for example). So I could not role-play it in a belivable way. Taking a fictional character and &quot;tweaking&quot; it a bit may seem like a good idea, but you have to admit: it sounds kinda cheap.<br /> <br /> <br /> (And on the note of creating flawed characters: most of mine are and I suppose that's because I - personally, in real life - consider myself flawed in many ways. Let's just say that my last character ended jumping from a certain bridge in Prague. But I suppose I shouldn't lie - it seems, that having a character that was flawed in the exact same way in which I - in my own opinion at least - am... sort of weighed on me. It certainly wasn't a good feeling, as it contributed to what I've said before - everytime I looked at him, it felt like I've been looking at myself from the side.)<br /> <br /> <br /> Anyways, thank you all for your answers. I may end up trying to play some already existing fictional character under a different alias, maybe looking different... Fortunately for me, I read quite a lot, so there's a chance to pick someone who the other players don't even know (not that they would have a problem with that, they are certainly more tolerant of what I'm doing than I myself am, which is kind of a paradox when you think about it).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4107,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4107-littledwarf","user":"LittleDwarf","date":". 10-18-2014, 02:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4107-littledwarf","name":"LittleDwarf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-10-18T12:52:07-07:00","id":286115,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LittleDwarf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/284289-how-to-separate-yourself-from-your-character#post284289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I said that I tend to create either clones of myself, or idealized versions of myself. The latter is only partially true. The thing is... I can't seem to be able to roleplay a character I'd REALLY want to be like in real life, just because... well... I am not like this. Example: I, in real life, am a very quiet, shy person. And I find it impossible to roleplay as an outgoing, boisterous guy 'cause even if I wanted to, I don't really know how those people act. I think it would seem... artificial.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe it would, maybe not. But that's how we learn. Right when we're born, we start out our learning via observation, and imitation.<br /> When you first start out imitating something, sometimes you end up as a caricature, stereotyped, or exaggerated, version of what you're imitating.<br /> Then you get better.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LittleDwarf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/284289-how-to-separate-yourself-from-your-character?p=286040#post286040\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd like to create a character that has mostly the same moral views as I do, but a distinctly different personality, so that it wouldn't instantly be reckognized as &quot;LittleDwarf with superpowers!&quot; A confident, out-spoken guy, for example. But I don't really... Well... I don't know if any of you actually understand that, but when I tried, I found out that I - in all seriousnes, out-of-character, in real life - actually DON'T KNOW what should be said or done in some situations (I am frequently left speechless when the conversations hit certain topics I am not used to speak about, for example). So I could not role-play it in a belivable way. Taking a fictional character and &quot;tweaking&quot; it a bit may seem like a good idea, but you have to admit: it sounds kinda cheap.</div> </div> </div> </div>When these other posters are advising you about how they use other characters as their inspiration, they as talking about what types of personality they are imitating. It's only 'cheap' when you do something like try to just insert the character whole-cloth into a different situation. <br /> And you might not know how to act in these ways, on-the-spot, like the characters do, because the writers had the opportunity to sit and think it all out, and you don't when playing.<br /> <br /> Basically, IMO, you are letting your self-confidence tell you not to try, because you will fail. And yes, you will. But you get better at it, and then, you don't fail as much. The thing is, the people you are playing with will fail at things too, and pretty often. Also, they'll fail at noticing when you fail. Humans are great at that. Failure is not a reason to not to try, it is a reason to learn how to fail less.<br /> Sorry if that comes across as an empty platitude, or otherwise cliche, but it is true.<br /> <br /> Maybe, ... Try to think of it as using the game group as a thought experiment. Trying to explore different versions of you in a safe environment where it doesn't matter as much if you fail or mess up, since the point of the experiment is to do exactly that in order to figure out how to do this correctly.<br /> <br /> Gaming is supposed to be fun, and it sounds like you're having trouble with that because of some personal issues, but maybe by emulating more of what you think you want to be, you might be able to have fun as you explore and figure out what that is and how to be it. And, maybe, hopefully, have fun as you do so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><i>&quot;Maturity is knowing you were an idiot in the past.&quot;<br /> &quot;If that's true, then Wisdom is knowing you'll be an idiot in the future.&quot;<br /> &quot;And Common Sense is knowing that you should try not to be an idiot NOW.&quot;<br /> -Questionable Content 976</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1042-zhyzhaksmate","name":"ZhyzhaksMate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1042_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-07T05:25:12-08:00","id":362350,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LittleDwarf</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/284289-how-to-separate-yourself-from-your-character#post284289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hi there.<br /> <br /> First of all, I'd like to say two things:<br /> <br /> 1] English isn't my native language so at times I may sound strange or my message may not be very clear. I apologize in advance for that.<br /> <br /> 2] Do not write anything if you intend on mocking me. I am mocking myself enough, I really don't need your &quot;support&quot; in that case. Unless mocking someone who you've never met over the Internet makes you feel very good about yourself, then who am I to deny you that.<br /> <br /> <br /> My question is... How do you separate yourself from your character when playing WoD? And is it really an issue for you at all, or am I just... weird?<br /> <br /> The thing is... some of my best friends play WoD regularly, and they are really nice, mature people who are great to play with. They don't seem to mind me, and I respect and like them a lot, so it would be nice to spend some time together when playing.<br /> <br /> I also like the setting very much - the closeness between the world of the game and the real world gives it this... sort of a &quot;real-ness&quot; feeling, makes you very engaged in the play, makes you feel very... strong emotions, as it is much easier to relate to the events that take place in the world that is mostly like ours, only with some &quot;additions&quot;. And that's great, I think. Especially for people who, like me, play mainly for the narrative.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> But that's... kind of a problem. For me at least. Whenever I try to play WoD, I just end up creating a supernatural version of myself. Or - in the least - the idealized, supernatural version of who I'd like to be but am not. And that... well, to me, that sucks. 'Cause when playing, I am... sort of constantly looking at myself from the side. And I don't really... like the things I see. Suffice to say, at the moment I don't... have a high opinion of myself. Worse still, I feel... exposed. Like... I am worried that the other people see what I see what I look at my character - which really, more often than not is just me - and see what I see. And then think less of me.<br /> <br /> <br /> I said that I tend to create either clones of myself, or idealized versions of myself. The latter is only partially true. The thing is... I can't seem to be able to roleplay a character I'd REALLY want to be like in real life, just because... well... I am not like this. Example: I, in real life, am a very quiet, shy person. And I find it impossible to roleplay as an outgoing, boisterous guy 'cause even if I wanted to, I don't really know how those people act. I think it would seem... artificial. And pathetic. But then, so is my own image - at least in my own eyes - and that's what I'm stuck with.<br /> <br /> <br /> Does anyone have this kind of problem as well? What did you do with it? (Also, as a half-serious note: do you feel stupid as well when your GM sets the base of the campaign on some historical events/seemingly common knowledge about the world in general, and you find out that you don't really know much about any of these things?)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Everyone who's new to gaming has their Mary Sue/Marty Stu phase. That's fine... really it is... Just learn to recognize the Cream-filled behavior when others call you out on it and gracefully take the criticism to dial it down a bit. What makes memorable and good characters is that they are flawed, they have weaknesses, make mistakes and they are not always perfect. Same goes for the horribly flawed characters, remember that they have virtues to build on as well. Players who can maturely develop a character that isn't an idealized alter ego of themselves or a clone of how they think themselves to be, they all have to start out with the Mary Sue/Marty Stu grow from there. There's a lot of this found in online RPGs. Some people don't have access to a regular gaming group to teach them how to actually socialize themselves and their characters, but they do have access to the internet. For that reason the online gaming can see a higher percentage of these kinds of under-socialized and overenthusiastic players than the normal usually singular 'Special Snowflake&quot; player that gives you and the ST headaches every group session.<br /> <br /> From what it sounds like though, you don't seem to have this problem. You play what you know, just as writers often write what they know or wish they knew. The way you can use those characters is to learn about yourself. You might not like what you see, but in that way, you can recognize your own flaws and start to change you from your characters little by little. I'll give you an example. I made a character much like myself, except better because she was a strong leader who was nice, firm, but fair. Where I was shy, insecure and let people bully me. I put up with things that were wrong morally because I didn't have the spine to speak up for myself and separate myself from the negative influences. I was ostrisized and ridiculed. I made a character who was tougher than me and even though she was small and shy too, her calling meant she had to stand up for herself and make herself heard. Somehow she got much more popular and liked in an online setting than I ever was in real life. So I tried to be more like her. Nice to people, tough, but fair, stronger like her than I was myself. I stopped getting walked all over, I spoke up for myself in a tough, but fair way and I held my head up. I kicked people out of my life who put me down and hung out with other people who had their lives more together so I could learn how to keep my life together. I began to be liked and appreciated for who I was, even if I still felt painfully shy and awkward. It wasn't until much later that I realized I'd actually used the character in the online games to better myself instead of making the character into an Ideal Me. I turned out better than my own character. It took me a while, a few years of playing, but when I had grown beyond this character and needing her to think on and learn to cope with life's challenges, I moved on to new characters that I could separate myself from much easier.<br /> <br /> I think it's perfectly normal and fine to have this phase of gaming, so long as your learn more about yourself through the experience. Once you see the parts of your personality or behaviors that are less than ideal, you can start to change yourself.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/31/25ew.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><a href=\"http://glyphery.webs.com/\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ut0SZ4h.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1332-darling_rose","name":"Darling_Rose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1332_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"434"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-01-19T06:15:40-08:00","id":375207,"title":"","content":"I ended creating a character that is... well, if I am to belive my fellow-players, he is substaintialy different from my last character, at least at first glance. Not always in the places I wanted him to, but well. That's a start, I guess. The whole group is very supportive and tolerant - and frankly I never expected less of them, 'cause they are wonderful people. But still...<br /> <br /> <br /> Sadly, it seems as I have always worried - that this whole... thing... of mine has in fact very little to do with the things I do in game, choices I make when playing, characters I choose to play. It all boils down to my situation in real life at the moment. Which is... not the best, really.<br /> <br /> So now I wonder if it is worth it. I mean - games should be fun. If they, for some reason, aren't - or as in my case, they ARE, but at the same time make you feel other, less... pleasent emotions - it all becomes a question of scale. On one hand - those are all very nice people I use to play with, and I'd love to spend more time with them. There is also the argument that the game is - after all - a lot safer and more secure &quot;training ground&quot; for certain social stances and activities than the real world is. On the other - I am a man who remembers... very well. Especially things I consider my own failures which, frankly, are a lot of things. And as I said before - the closeness between WoD's setting and the real life leaves me... unable to stop making connections. Comparisons. And drawing conclusions from them. About me and who I am, mostly. Who I am not and seem at the moment never to be able to be. But also about what the people must think of me. Most of my friends would say they are the wrong conclusions, but it's not like I'm making them up the way they are on purpose.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Nevertheless, I'd like to thank you all. The Internet is... well, it is a mixed bag for the most part. It is delightfully easy to mock people over the Internet and I am sure that in some circles I would be a perfect target because, c'mon, what sort of a wuss has this kind of problems with a GAME. But it seems that I was correct - I thought - partially judging from my own gaming group, who are a couple of years older than me - that WoD in particular is a gaming system that... well, isn't really a first choice for the people who &quot;just want to hack stuff and earn XP&quot;. And when you stop for a moment and start thinking... I found out it becames a lot more difficult to carelessly say things that may hurt others. So thanks again, you are a very nice, mature community and it is refreshing to find one in this sea of filth we call the Internet.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4107-littledwarf","name":"LittleDwarf","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}